        "prompt": "Profile KernelGen round_num=1 (the only measured round) for operator cumulative_trapezoid.\n\nContext: the candidate is the unmodified seed. It launches ONE Triton kernel per call: one program per (outer, inner) pair, BLOCK=next_power_of_2(N-1), single-program 1D `tl.cumsum(t, axis=0)` in FP32, then a separate host-side multiply by dx when dx != 1 (not used by the timing inputs, all of which have dx=1).\n\nAuthoritative measurements (geo_mean 0.3535):\n- timing-0 fp16 [128,1024] dim=-1: cand 0.1128 ms vs ref 0.0652\n- timing-1 fp16 [1024,4096] dim=-1: cand 1.7151 ms vs ref 0.2618  (worst, 0.153x)\n- timing-2 fp16 [1024,128] dim=0:   cand 0.2414 ms vs ref 0.0543\n- timing-3 bf16 [128,1024] dim=-1: cand 0.1076 ms vs ref 0.0579\n- timing-4 bf16 [1024,4096] dim=-1: cand 1.7145 ms vs ref 0.3175\n- timing-5 bf16 [1024,128] dim=0:   cand 0.2412 ms vs ref 0.3007 (only >1x case)\n- timing-6 fp32 [128,1024] dim=-1: cand 0.1264 ms vs ref 0.0681\n- timing-7 fp32 [1024,4096] dim=-1: cand 1.7101 ms vs ref 0.3172\n- timing-8 fp32 [1024,128] dim=0:   cand 0.1646 ms vs ref 0.0576\n\nMy competing explanations for the 4096-length 1.7 ms:\n(H1) the Ascend lowering of `tl.cumsum` on a 1D 4096-element block is not a log-step vector scan but a long scalar/serial loop or a transpose-based serial path, so per-element cost is ~15 cycles;\n(H2) UB pressure / spill with four 4096-element FP32 live blocks (a, b, t, cs = 64 KB) in a 192 KB UB;\n(H3) the kernel is fine per element but MTE2 (GM->UB) traffic with uncoalesced/masked loads dominates;\n(H4) host launch / synchronization overhead dominates rather than the device kernel.\n\nPriorities, in order:\n1. Confirm or reject H1 by looking at the actual generated IR / instruction listing for the fp16 [1024,4096] case (or the closest available): is `tl.cumsum`/`tl.associative_scan` lowered to a bounded number of whole-block vector adds (log2(N) steps), or to a per-element serial loop / scalarized ops? Quantify the instruction count if possible.\n2. Report the device kernel time vs the full-call walltime for that case (how much is kernel vs host), and for the small [128,1024] case (0.113 ms total) as well — H4 matters most for the small shapes.\n3. Report pipeline utilization (Vector / Scalar / MTE2 / MTE3) and GM bytes for the big case, so I can tell whether it is compute-bound or transfer-bound (H3).\n4. Note whether UB usage looks near the limit (H2).\n\nThen give me exactly ONE actionable next experiment for the Coder. The decision I need to make: for the dim=-1 case, should I (a) keep `tl.cumsum` but restructure it as a 2D [BLOCK_M, BLOCK_K] block scanned along axis=1 (amortizing the scan over many rows per program), (b) abandon `tl.cumsum` for a manual log-step shifted-add scan over a 2D block, or (c) something else the evidence points to. If the scan is already log-step and cheap, then the bottleneck is elsewhere (tiles/launch) and say so.\n\nDo not modify tmp/main.py or any evaluation state. Read-only evidence collection."
